Auxiliary gradients prevent the vision encoder from overfitting to the action prediction objective, preserving more general spatial features in the DINOv2 and SigLIP representations. Multi-task learning theory predicts this effect: shared representations trained on multiple objectives retain broader feature diversity than those specialized to a single task~\cite{caruana1997multitask, ruder2017overview}. Lippl and Lindsey~\cite{lippl2024implicit} show that multi-task training in overparameterized networks induces $\ell_{1,2}$ mixed-norm regularization that encourages feature sharing, while single-task fine-tuning exhibits nested feature selection that discards unused pretrained features. Under this hypothesis, Random OCC and Full OCC both regularize because both supply non-trivial gradients through the encoder---the label content matters less than the gradient signal itself. The observation that Full OCC (0.797) and Random OCC (0.789) achieve nearly identical CAR (0.8pp difference, 3 seeds each) is consistent with this account. However, the current data more strongly support a ``damage prevention'' framing than a ``positive regularization'' framing: gradient-flow conditions do not clearly exceed Vanilla, but their absence clearly harms performance.

